## Basic Entity

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "orders", schema = "commerce")
public class Order {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(name = "order_number", nullable = false, unique = true, length = 50)
    private String orderNumber;

    @Column(name = "total_amount", precision = 19, scale = 4)
    private BigDecimal totalAmount;

    @Column(name = "created_at", nullable = false, updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)  // Store as string, not ordinal
    @Column(nullable = false)
    private OrderStatus status;

    protected Order() { }  // For Hibernate

    public Order(String orderNumber, BigDecimal total) {
        this.orderNumber = orderNumber;
        this.totalAmount = total;
        this.status = OrderStatus.PENDING;
        this.createdAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }
    // Getters...
}
```

## @GeneratedValue Strategies

| Strategy | Mechanism | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| `IDENTITY` | DB auto-increment | MySQL, MariaDB (but disables batch insert) |
| `SEQUENCE` | DB sequence object | PostgreSQL, Oracle |
| `TABLE` | JPA-managed table | Portable but slow |
| `AUTO` | Provider chooses | Development only |

## @EnumType

Always use `EnumType.STRING` in production:
```java
// Bad — ORDINAL (default)
@Enumerated(EnumType.ORDINAL)  // Stores 0, 1, 2...
// Adding enum value in the middle breaks existing data!

// Good — STRING
@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)  // Stores "PENDING", "CONFIRMED"...
// Adding enum values anywhere is safe
```

## Production Reality

Use `SEQUENCE` strategy with PostgreSQL for batch insert performance. `IDENTITY` requires the database to generate the ID and return it for each insert — this prevents Hibernate from batching multiple inserts into one statement. `SEQUENCE` lets Hibernate pre-fetch a range of IDs and batch inserts freely.

## Entity States

```
New/Transient    → persist() →   Managed
                 ← remove() ←    ↓↑ flush/commit (dirty checking)
Detached         ← detach()  ←   Managed
Detached         → merge()   →   Managed
Removed          ← remove()  ←   Managed
                 → commit/rollback → Detached (removed from DB)
```

## Dirty Checking in Practice

```java
@Transactional
public void updateUserEmail(Long userId, String newEmail) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(userId).orElseThrow();
    // user is now MANAGED — in the persistence context

    user.setEmail(newEmail);  // Change a field
    // That's it — no userRepository.save(user) needed!

    // At transaction commit:
    // Hibernate compares current state to snapshot
    // Generates: UPDATE users SET email = ? WHERE id = ?
    // Executes it automatically
}
```

## First-Level Cache

```java
@Transactional
public void demonstrateCache() {
    User a = userRepository.findById(1L).orElseThrow();
    User b = userRepository.findById(1L).orElseThrow();  // Second call

    // No second DB query! b is the same Java object from the PC
    assertSame(a, b);  // True — same object reference
}
```

## Detached Entities

```java
@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public User loadUser(Long id) {
    return userRepository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    // Method returns, transaction commits → user becomes DETACHED
}

public void modifyDetached() {
    User user = loadUser(1L);  // Now detached
    user.setEmail("new@example.com");  // NOT tracked — won't persist!

    // To persist changes on a detached entity:
    userService.saveDetached(user);  // Must explicitly save/merge
}
```

## Production Reality

Understanding dirty checking prevents two common bugs: (1) forgetting to call `save()` when it IS needed (detached entity), and (2) thinking `save()` is always needed and calling it unnecessarily (managed entity). In a managed context, if you load-then-modify inside a `@Transactional` method, the save is automatic.

## JPQL vs SQL

```java
// SQL
// SELECT u.id, u.name, u.email FROM users u
// WHERE u.is_active = true AND u.created_at > '2024-01-01'

// JPQL equivalent
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.active = true AND u.createdAt > :since")
List<User> findActiveUsersSince(@Param("since") LocalDate since);
```

## JPQL in Repository

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // JPQL — navigates entity relationships
    @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.user.email = :email AND o.status = :status")
    List<Order> findByUserEmailAndStatus(
        @Param("email") String email,
        @Param("status") OrderStatus status
    );

    // JPQL with JOIN FETCH (avoid N+1)
    @Query("SELECT o FROM Order o JOIN FETCH o.items WHERE o.id = :id")
    Optional<Order> findByIdWithItems(@Param("id") Long id);

    // Projection — select specific fields
    @Query("SELECT u.name AS name, u.email AS email FROM User u WHERE u.id = :id")
    Optional<UserSummary> findSummaryById(@Param("id") Long id);

    // Native SQL when JPQL can't express it
    @Query(value = "SELECT * FROM orders WHERE total_amount > :min FOR UPDATE",
           nativeQuery = true)
    List<Order> findForUpdateNative(@Param("min") BigDecimal min);
}
```

## JPQL Syntax Differences from SQL

| SQL | JPQL |
|---|---|
| `FROM users u` | `FROM User u` |
| `u.email_address` | `u.emailAddress` |
| `INNER JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id` | `JOIN u.orders o` (uses mapped relationship) |
| `COUNT(*)` | `COUNT(u)` |
| `WHERE u.type = 'ADMIN'` | `WHERE TYPE(u) = Admin` (for inheritance) |

## Named Parameters vs Positional

```java
// Named (preferred — readable)
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = :email")
Optional<User> findByEmail(@Param("email") String email);

// Positional (order matters — fragile)
@Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.email = ?1")
Optional<User> findByEmailPositional(String email);
```

## Production Reality

Prefer JPQL over native SQL for portability and mapping. Use native SQL only when you need database-specific features. When you do use native SQL in Spring Data, be careful: it returns `Object[]` or requires a result set mapping — whereas JPQL automatically maps results to entities or projections.

## Raw Criteria API

```java
@Repository
public class OrderSearchRepository {

    @PersistenceContext
    private EntityManager em;

    public List<Order> search(OrderSearchCriteria criteria) {
        CriteriaBuilder cb = em.getCriteriaBuilder();
        CriteriaQuery<Order> cq = cb.createQuery(Order.class);
        Root<Order> root = cq.from(Order.class);

        List<Predicate> predicates = new ArrayList<>();

        if (criteria.getUserEmail() != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("user").get("email"), criteria.getUserEmail()));
        }
        if (criteria.getStatus() != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.equal(root.get("status"), criteria.getStatus()));
        }
        if (criteria.getMinAmount() != null) {
            predicates.add(cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("totalAmount"), criteria.getMinAmount()));
        }

        cq.where(predicates.toArray(new Predicate[0]));
        cq.orderBy(cb.desc(root.get("createdAt")));

        return em.createQuery(cq)
            .setMaxResults(50)
            .getResultList();
    }
}
```

## Specification Pattern (Cleaner)

```java
public class OrderSpecifications {

    public static Specification<Order> hasStatus(OrderStatus status) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            status == null ? null : cb.equal(root.get("status"), status);
    }

    public static Specification<Order> hasMinAmount(BigDecimal min) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            min == null ? null : cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("totalAmount"), min);
    }

    public static Specification<Order> createdAfter(LocalDate date) {
        return (root, query, cb) ->
            date == null ? null : cb.greaterThanOrEqualTo(root.get("createdAt"), date.atStartOfDay());
    }
}
```

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long>,
        JpaSpecificationExecutor<Order> { }

// Usage
public Page<Order> search(OrderSearchRequest req, Pageable pageable) {
    Specification<Order> spec = where(hasStatus(req.getStatus()))
        .and(hasMinAmount(req.getMinAmount()))
        .and(createdAfter(req.getSince()));

    return orderRepository.findAll(spec, pageable);
}
```

## Production Reality

Use `Specification` over raw Criteria API — it's more readable and composable. For complex search APIs with many optional filters, Specification is the right tool. For simple queries, `@Query` with JPQL is cleaner. For very complex reporting queries, consider native SQL or a query builder like jOOQ.

## Callbacks in the Entity

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;
    private BigDecimal price;

    @Column(updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @PrePersist
    protected void onCreate() {
        createdAt = LocalDateTime.now();
        updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    @PreUpdate
    protected void onUpdate() {
        updatedAt = LocalDateTime.now();
    }

    @PostLoad
    protected void onLoad() {
        // Calculate derived fields after loading
    }
}
```

## External EntityListener

```java
public class AuditEntityListener {
    @PrePersist
    @PreUpdate
    public void audit(Object entity) {
        if (entity instanceof Auditable auditable) {
            auditable.setUpdatedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
            if (auditable.getCreatedAt() == null) {
                auditable.setCreatedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
            }
        }
    }
}

@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditEntityListener.class)
public class Order implements Auditable { ... }
```

## Spring Data Auditing (Preferred)

```java
@Configuration
@EnableJpaAuditing(auditorAwareRef = "auditorProvider")
public class JpaConfig {
    @Bean
    public AuditorAware<String> auditorProvider() {
        return () -> Optional.ofNullable(SecurityContextHolder.getContext()
            .getAuthentication())
            .map(Authentication::getName);
    }
}

@Entity
@EntityListeners(AuditingEntityListener.class)
public class Order {
    @CreatedDate
    @Column(updatable = false)
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;

    @LastModifiedDate
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;

    @CreatedBy
    @Column(updatable = false)
    private String createdBy;

    @LastModifiedBy
    private String updatedBy;
}
```

## Production Reality

Prefer Spring Data's `@CreatedDate`/`@LastModifiedDate` over manual `@PrePersist`/`@PreUpdate`. Spring's auditing works with Hibernate envers and is more feature-complete. Only use raw callbacks for logic that doesn't fit the auditing pattern — like computed fields or validation on persist.

## @Version Implementation

```java
@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    private String name;
    private BigDecimal price;

    @Version  // Hibernate manages this automatically
    private Integer version;  // Or Long, Short, Timestamp
}
```

## How Hibernate Uses @Version

```sql
-- SELECT — no lock
SELECT id, name, price, version FROM products WHERE id = 1;
-- Returns: id=1, name="Widget", price=10.00, version=3

-- Thread A updates:
UPDATE products SET name=?, price=?, version=4 WHERE id=1 AND version=3;
-- 1 row affected → success, version now 4

-- Thread B also had version=3, tries to update:
UPDATE products SET name=?, price=?, version=4 WHERE id=1 AND version=3;
-- 0 rows affected (version is now 4, not 3)
-- → OptimisticLockException thrown!
```

## Handling OptimisticLockException

```java
@Service
public class ProductService {

    @Retryable(value = OptimisticLockException.class, maxAttempts = 3)
    @Transactional
    public Product updatePrice(Long id, BigDecimal newPrice) {
        Product product = repository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
        product.setPrice(newPrice);
        return product;  // Dirty checking saves it
    }

    // Manual handling
    @Transactional
    public Product updatePriceWithConflictCheck(Long id, BigDecimal newPrice, Integer expectedVersion) {
        try {
            Product product = repository.findById(id).orElseThrow();
            if (!product.getVersion().equals(expectedVersion)) {
                throw new ConflictException("Product was modified — please refresh and retry");
            }
            product.setPrice(newPrice);
            return product;
        } catch (OptimisticLockException e) {
            throw new ConflictException("Concurrent modification detected");
        }
    }
}
```

## Production Reality

Expose the `version` field in your API responses for resources that users can edit. When the client sends an update, include the version. If it doesn't match, return 409 Conflict. This gives the client a chance to refresh the data and retry. For background processes, use `@Retryable` with exponential backoff — retry a few times before failing the operation.

## Repository Method

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Automatic pagination support
    Page<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);

    // JPQL with pagination
    @Query(value = "SELECT o FROM Order o WHERE o.user.email = :email ORDER BY o.createdAt DESC",
           countQuery = "SELECT COUNT(o) FROM Order o WHERE o.user.email = :email")
    Page<Order> findByUserEmail(@Param("email") String email, Pageable pageable);

    // Slice — no count query (faster, but no total pages info)
    Slice<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status, Pageable pageable);
}
```

## Service Layer

```java
@Service
public class OrderService {

    public Page<OrderDTO> getOrdersPage(OrderFilter filter, int page, int size, String sortBy) {
        Sort sort = Sort.by(Sort.Direction.DESC, sortBy);
        Pageable pageable = PageRequest.of(page, size, sort);

        Page<Order> orders = orderRepository.findByStatus(filter.getStatus(), pageable);
        return orders.map(orderMapper::toDTO);
    }
}
```

## Controller Integration

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderController {

    @GetMapping
    public ResponseEntity<PageResponse<OrderDTO>> getOrders(
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "0") int page,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "20") int size,
            @RequestParam(defaultValue = "createdAt") String sort) {

        Page<OrderDTO> result = orderService.getOrdersPage(page, size, sort);

        return ResponseEntity.ok(new PageResponse<>(
            result.getContent(),
            result.getNumber(),
            result.getTotalPages(),
            result.getTotalElements()
        ));
    }
}
```

## Slice vs Page

| | `Page<T>` | `Slice<T>` |
|---|---|---|
| Total count query | Yes | No |
| `hasNext()` | From count | From actual results |
| Performance | Slower | Faster |
| Use for | Paginated lists with page numbers | Infinite scroll |

## Production Reality

Limit maximum page size in the API — don't let clients request thousands of records per page. Validate `size <= 100` at the controller level. For very large datasets, cursor-based pagination (using last-seen ID) is more efficient than offset-based because `OFFSET 10000` still scans 10,000 rows even if they're discarded.

## @NamedEntityGraph on Entity

```java
@Entity
@NamedEntityGraph(
    name = "Order.withItems",
    attributeNodes = {
        @NamedAttributeNode("items"),
        @NamedAttributeNode(value = "user", subgraph = "user-details")
    },
    subgraphs = {
        @NamedSubgraph(
            name = "user-details",
            attributeNodes = @NamedAttributeNode("address")
        )
    }
)
public class Order {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "order", fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private List<OrderItem> items;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    private User user;
}
```

## Using in Repository

```java
public interface OrderRepository extends JpaRepository<Order, Long> {

    // Load with named graph
    @EntityGraph("Order.withItems")
    Optional<Order> findById(Long id);

    // Inline graph definition
    @EntityGraph(attributePaths = {"items", "user", "user.address"})
    List<Order> findByStatus(OrderStatus status);

    // Without graph — items and user remain LAZY
    List<Order> findByCreatedAtAfter(LocalDateTime since);
}
```

## Generated SQL

```sql
-- Without entity graph (lazy)
SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'PENDING';
-- Then: N separate queries for each order's items!

-- With entity graph
SELECT o.*, i.*, u.*
FROM orders o
LEFT JOIN order_items i ON i.order_id = o.id
LEFT JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id
WHERE o.status = 'PENDING';
-- One query, everything loaded
```

## Fetch Type vs Load Type

```java
// FETCH graph: ONLY load specified attributes eagerly; rest defaults to LAZY
@EntityGraph(type = FETCH, attributePaths = "items")
// items: EAGER; user: LAZY (regardless of mapping default)

// LOAD graph: load specified eagerly; rest uses their MAPPING defaults
@EntityGraph(type = LOAD, attributePaths = "items")
// items: EAGER; user: whatever FetchType.* is set on the mapping
```

## Production Reality

Entity graphs are valuable when you need to efficiently load related data for specific use cases without changing global defaults. But avoid fetching too many associations at once — a Cartesian product JOIN with many collections can return massive result sets. When loading three `@OneToMany` associations simultaneously, Hibernate returns `n1 × n2 × n3` rows before deduplicating — consider multiple queries instead.

## Interface Projection

```java
// Define interface
public interface UserSummary {
    Long getId();
    String getName();
    String getEmail();
    // Spring generates: SELECT u.id, u.name, u.email FROM users u
}

// Repository method
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    List<UserSummary> findByActiveTrue();
    Optional<UserSummary> findSummaryById(Long id);
}
```

## Class (DTO) Projection

```java
// DTO class
public record UserSummaryDTO(Long id, String name, String email) { }

// Repository with JPQL constructor expression
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    @Query("SELECT new com.example.dto.UserSummaryDTO(u.id, u.name, u.email) FROM User u WHERE u.active = true")
    List<UserSummaryDTO> findActiveSummaries();
}
```

## Nested Interface Projection

```java
public interface OrderSummary {
    Long getId();
    String getStatus();
    UserInfo getUser();  // Nested

    interface UserInfo {
        String getName();
        String getEmail();
    }
}
// Generated: SELECT o.id, o.status, u.name, u.email FROM orders o JOIN users u ON...
```

## Open Projection (SpEL)

```java
public interface UserProfile {
    @Value("#{target.firstName + ' ' + target.lastName}")
    String getFullName();  // Combined at runtime — loads full entity!

    // Closed projection (no SpEL) — efficient, only fetches used columns
    String getEmail();
}
```

## Production Reality

Use closed interface projections or DTO projections for read-heavy endpoints that don't need the full entity. For write operations, always work with the full entity to ensure dirty checking works correctly. A common pattern: one repository method returns `Page<OrderSummary>` for list endpoints, another returns `Optional<Order>` for detail/edit endpoints.

## SINGLE_TABLE

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "payment_type", discriminatorType = DiscriminatorType.STRING)
public abstract class Payment {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private BigDecimal amount;
    private LocalDateTime processedAt;
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("CREDIT_CARD")
public class CreditCardPayment extends Payment {
    private String cardLastFour;
    private String cardBrand;  // Nullable for non-CC payments
}

@Entity
@DiscriminatorValue("BANK_TRANSFER")
public class BankTransferPayment extends Payment {
    private String iban;  // Nullable for non-BT payments
}
```

```sql
-- Single table:
CREATE TABLE payment (
  id BIGINT, amount DECIMAL, processed_at TIMESTAMP,
  payment_type VARCHAR,  -- discriminator
  card_last_four VARCHAR,  -- nullable
  card_brand VARCHAR,      -- nullable
  iban VARCHAR             -- nullable
);
```

## JOINED

```java
@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.JOINED)
public abstract class Notification {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;
    private String message;
    private String userId;
}

@Entity
public class EmailNotification extends Notification {
    private String toAddress;
    private String subject;
}

@Entity
public class SmsNotification extends Notification {
    private String phoneNumber;
}
```

```sql
-- Three tables:
-- notification(id, message, user_id)
-- email_notification(id, to_address, subject)  [id FK → notification.id]
-- sms_notification(id, phone_number)           [id FK → notification.id]

-- Query for EmailNotification:
SELECT n.*, e.* FROM notification n
INNER JOIN email_notification e ON n.id = e.id
WHERE ...;
```

## Strategy Comparison

| | SINGLE_TABLE | JOINED | TABLE_PER_CLASS |
|---|---|---|---|
| Tables | 1 | n+1 | n |
| Read performance | Best | Good (JOIN) | Good (concrete) |
| Polymorphic query | Best | Good | Worst (UNION) |
| Normalization | Poor | Best | Poor |
| Nullable columns | Yes | No | No |

## Production Reality

For small class hierarchies with similar fields, SINGLE_TABLE is simplest and fastest. For deeply different subclasses where nullable columns would be many, JOINED is cleaner. Avoid TABLE_PER_CLASS — limited support in JPA providers and UNION queries are rarely optimal. If subclasses diverge significantly, consider separate unrelated entities instead of inheritance mapping.
